WEBVTT

00:00:00.502 --> 00:00:03.024
Touch is essential for precise manipulation.

00:00:03.815 --> 00:00:06.776
Yet adding touch to a pretrained VLA policy

00:00:06.689 --> 00:00:09.430
like π₀.₅ remains challenging.

00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:12.459
Tactile policy pretraining can help,

00:00:12.526 --> 00:00:14.765
but requires extra data and training.

00:00:15.555 --> 00:00:17.613
SimpleTouch explores another route.

00:00:18.403 --> 00:00:22.111
Can vision-language-action models master contact-rich manipulation

00:00:22.024 --> 00:00:24.070
without tactile policy pretraining?

00:00:26.062 --> 00:00:30.118
SimpleTouch combines a pretrained tactile encoder with a tactile expert.

00:00:30.908 --> 00:00:33.534
It learns from fifty demonstrations per task,

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:36.266
without additional tactile policy pretraining.

00:00:37.055 --> 00:00:40.209
The chart on the right summarizes SimpleTouch's performance.

00:00:40.999 --> 00:00:44.282
It achieves the highest success rate on all ten tasks,

00:00:44.388 --> 00:00:48.302
covering six simulation tasks and four real-robot tasks.

00:00:49.432 --> 00:00:50.794
Here is how it works.

00:00:51.585 --> 00:00:54.829
We start with the pretrained π₀.₅ policy.

00:00:55.761 --> 00:00:57.459
We add one tactile expert

00:00:57.372 --> 00:01:00.126
to process features from a frozen tactile encoder.

00:01:00.808 --> 00:01:04.710
During training, this expert learns to predict future tactile features

00:01:04.622 --> 00:01:06.282
at multiple points in time.

00:01:06.963 --> 00:01:08.184
When the robot acts,

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:11.316
it uses only the current visual and tactile context.

00:01:12.107 --> 00:01:15.209
Future tactile prediction is needed only during training.

00:01:16.572 --> 00:01:18.501
Let's look at USB insertion.

00:01:19.292 --> 00:01:22.859
In the vision-only example, the connector is not fully seated.

00:01:23.648 --> 00:01:26.506
SimpleTouch uses touch to complete the insertion.

00:01:28.194 --> 00:01:29.892
The close-ups show the difference.

00:01:30.683 --> 00:01:32.883
SimpleTouch seats the connector fully.

00:01:36.102 --> 00:01:38.521
Fragile objects pose a different challenge.

00:01:39.312 --> 00:01:42.762
Here, vision alone leads to a missed grasp in one attempt

00:01:42.675 --> 00:01:44.206
and a broken chip in another.

00:01:44.995 --> 00:01:47.865
SimpleTouch grasps and lifts the chip intact.

00:01:51.052 --> 00:01:52.389
Next is Mahjong.

00:01:53.179 --> 00:01:55.071
First, the one-dot tile.

00:01:57.452 --> 00:01:59.859
SimpleTouch identifies the tile through touch

00:01:59.772 --> 00:02:01.483
and performs the winning action.

00:02:04.852 --> 00:02:06.807
Now, the one-bamboo tile.

00:02:09.189 --> 00:02:10.410
With a different tile,

00:02:10.413 --> 00:02:13.206
SimpleTouch selects the discard action instead.

00:02:16.572 --> 00:02:18.733
Finally, consider board wiping.

00:02:19.524 --> 00:02:21.557
From the wrist camera, we can see that

00:02:21.470 --> 00:02:24.057
the vision-only policy leaves visible residue.

00:02:24.846 --> 00:02:27.975
SimpleTouch maintains contact and leaves a cleaner board.

00:02:29.302 --> 00:02:32.146
Across the four real-world tasks you've just seen,

00:02:32.317 --> 00:02:34.814
SimpleTouch achieves an average success rate of

00:02:34.727 --> 00:02:36.592
71.3%,

00:02:36.698 --> 00:02:40.226
compared with 62.5% for FTP-1.

00:02:41.462 --> 00:02:44.255
We also evaluate SimpleTouch in simulation.

00:02:45.046 --> 00:02:47.465
On UniVTAC, SimpleTouch achieves

00:02:47.378 --> 00:02:50.919
an average success rate of 77.5%,

00:02:51.089 --> 00:02:54.089
with the highest success rate on all six tasks.

00:02:56.052 --> 00:02:58.832
Together, these results point to a simpler route

00:02:58.745 --> 00:03:00.597
to contact-rich manipulation.

00:03:01.388 --> 00:03:05.779
With a pretrained policy, a tactile encoder, and task demonstrations,

00:03:05.950 --> 00:03:07.661
SimpleTouch learns to use touch

00:03:07.574 --> 00:03:10.199
without additional tactile policy pretraining.

00:03:10.989 --> 00:03:12.248
Thank you for watching.
